## Proof

**Setup.** Real projective plane $\mathbb{RP}^2$ is the quotient of the square $[0,1]^2$ by the identifications
$$(0,y)\sim(1,1-y),\qquad (x,0)\sim(1-x,1),$$
i.e. opposite edges identified with a half-twist. Let $p$ be the image of the center of the square. Define
- $U_2$: a small open disk around $p$,
- $U_1$: the complement in $\mathbb{RP}^2$ of a slightly smaller closed disk $\overline{D}\subset U_2$ centered at $p$.

Then $U_1\cup U_2=\mathbb{RP}^2$ and $U_1\cap U_2$ is an open annulus, so Van Kampen applies.

**$\pi_1(U_2)$.** $U_2$ is an open disk, hence contractible:
$$\pi_1(U_2)=1.$$

**$\pi_1(U_1)$.** $U_1$ is the square with a small open disk removed around the center, with the boundary identifications on the outer square. The region between the inner circle (boundary of the removed disk) and the outer boundary deformation retracts onto the outer boundary. After the quotient identifications, the outer boundary of the square becomes the $1$-skeleton of $\mathbb{RP}^2$, which is $\mathbb{RP}^1\cong S^1$. Hence
$$\pi_1(U_1)\cong\mathbb{Z},\qquad \pi_1(U_1)=\langle a\rangle,$$
where $a$ is the generator represented by a single traversal of the $1$-skeleton $\mathbb{RP}^1$.

**$\pi_1(U_1\cap U_2)$.** The intersection is an open annulus, deformation retracting onto $S^1$:
$$\pi_1(U_1\cap U_2)\cong\mathbb{Z},\qquad \pi_1(U_1\cap U_2)=\langle c\rangle,$$
where $c$ is the loop going once around the inner circle (boundary of the excised disk), oriented as the boundary of $U_1$.

**The inclusion maps.** 

- $i_2:\pi_1(U_1\cap U_2)\to\pi_1(U_2)$: since $U_2$ is contractible, $i_2(c)=1$.

- $i_1:\pi_1(U_1\cap U_2)\to\pi_1(U_1)$: the loop $c$, being the boundary of the excised disk, is homotopic in $U_1$ to the boundary of the square. Traversing the boundary of the square once, under the twist identifications $(0,y)\sim(1,1-y)$ and $(x,0)\sim(1-x,1)$, wraps **twice** around the $1$-skeleton $\mathbb{RP}^1$ — this is exactly the degree-$2$ attaching map of the $2$-cell of $\mathbb{RP}^2$. Concretely: the bottom edge $(x,0)$, $x:0\to1$, is identified with the top edge traversed in reverse, and the left edge is identified with the right edge in reverse, so all four edges compose into a path that runs around $\mathbb{RP}^1$ two times. Therefore
$$i_1(c)=a^{2}.$$

**Applying Van Kampen.** With $U_1\cap U_2$ path-connected,
$$\pi_1(\mathbb{RP}^2)\cong \pi_1(U_1)*\pi_1(U_2)\Big/\big\langle\!\big\langle\, i_1(c)\,i_2(c)^{-1}\,\big\rangle\!\big\rangle = \langle a\rangle * 1 \Big/ \big\langle\!\big\langle\, a^{2}\,\big\rangle\!\big\rangle = \langle a\mid a^{2}=1\rangle.$$

**Conclusion.**
$$\boxed{\;\pi_1(\mathbb{RP}^2)\cong\mathbb{Z}/2\mathbb{Z}\;}$$

The fundamental group of the real projective plane is the cyclic group of order $2$.

